use /lib
use /ui
use /ui/components/sidebar/Sidebar
from ./tasks import TasksTab
from /scope/application/Body import Body
from /ui/components/scrollbar import Scrollbar
from /ui/components/sidebar import Sidebar

export sidebar RightSidebar:
    side: right

    panels:
        - @{TasksTab}

    pitch:
        A second @{Sidebar}, at the right of the message list and the
        chat input beneath it, holding the project's tasks, what has run,
        what is running, and what is queued, as the @{TasksTab} says.
        What the running task is working from, its referenced files,
        understanding, and subagents, is in the left sidebar's Run view.

    description:
        It runs the full height of the body, beside the body's tab bar, the
        message list or file beneath it, and the chat input beneath those,
        which stops at its left edge rather than running beneath it. It
        keeps the width it was dragged to while the application runs.

        It holds the @{TasksTab} alone, filling it, with no tab row
        along its top: the list begins directly beneath the sidebar's top
        edge, its section headings the first thing in it.

    look:
        The sidebar's body is on the ribbon's command area colour, #222222 in
        dark mode.        The only line is the one down its left edge, where it meets the
        message list and the chat input, which is the handle it is resized
        by, a line nowhere else. In light mode the
        same steps are taken, as the ribbon's are.

    showing:
        It is there while a task is running, from the moment a task is sent
        from the Code, Chain, or Spec tab until its run is over, and while the
        queue holds a prompt, and whenever it is opened by hand: a small
        ghost button with a sidebar icon at the right end of the body's tab
        bar, or Ctrl+Alt+B (Cmd+Alt+B on macOS), opens it,
        and closes it again, its tooltip saying which. Opened by hand, it
        stays until closed by hand, whatever runs; otherwise it slides back
        once nothing runs and nothing is queued. Questions from the Ask tab
        never show it, even when one is sent from the queue.

        While the body's Planning tab is selected, it isn't there at all,
        whatever runs, is queued, or was opened by hand, as the @{Body} says,
        and the tab bar's sidebar button and Ctrl+Alt+B (Cmd+Alt+B on macOS)
        do nothing. It goes and comes back at once, with no slide, keeping its
        width and whether it was opened by hand; when another tab is selected
        it is there or not as the rules above say for that moment.

        When a task starts, it slides out from the right edge of the message
        list and the chat input, growing from nothing to its width, its
        contents sliding into view from behind that edge as it does. When the
        run is over, it slides back the same way. The slide is quick and
        settles without bouncing. It pushes rather than covers: from the
        first frame of the slide to the last, the body's tab bar, the message
        list or open file beneath it, and the chat input end exactly at the
        sidebar's left edge, narrowing as it grows and widening as it
        shrinks, laid out again each frame, as the @{Body} says. Nothing
        runs beneath the sliding sidebar, nothing is clipped by it, and
        nothing jumps to a new width once the slide settles.

from ./referenced-files export ReferencedFilesPanel
from ./understanding export UnderstandingPanel
from ./subagents export SubagentsPanel

export TasksTab
